## General lessons worth carrying forward (from the harness this template was exported from)

These are process-level lessons, not project-specific facts — worth keeping
in mind as this new harness accumulates its own incidents:

- **A rule with no incident behind it tends to erode under time pressure.**
  Rules that sound obviously right in the abstract get argued down or
  quietly skipped in a real crunch; a rule with a one-line "this is what it
  cost us last time" note behind it survives. Populate this file's
  provenance section for real as incidents happen here, rather than leaving
  it as an abstract rulebook.
- **Mechanical backstops beat honor-system rules for anything checkable.**
  Line caps, provenance sidecars, and role attribution on recorded work all
  eventually got a small script backing them, because the honor-system
  version recurred despite everyone agreeing it mattered. If a rule can be
  checked by a script, build the script sooner rather than later.
- **"Done" needs a mechanical definition, not a vibe.** A closeout checklist
  (the work record exists, the artifact exists in the right place, the task
  moved with that record's reference pasted next to it, the Verify line
  actually re-run) catches fabricated-looking claims that a plain "looks
  done to me" review misses.
- **A dead monitor next to a live job is worse than no monitor** — it reads
  as reassurance. Heartbeat staleness needs an explicit "too old = treat as
  dead" threshold, not an implicit one.
- **Detached ≠ crash-proof** in every environment. If your session runs
  inside a container/cgroup-managed multiplexer, standard shell-level
  detachment (`setsid`/`nohup`/`disown`) protects against SIGHUP but not
  against the whole cgroup being torn down — verify what your actual
  execution environment does on a crash before trusting "it's backgrounded"
  as durability.
- **Placement is verified after launch, never inferred from the flag you
  set.** A device pin can silently select a different physical device than
  intended (runtimes and system tools disagree on index ordering; a wrong
  index looks identical in the log), and the symptom is not an error — it's
  a job that merely runs slow, or OOMs hours later. Cross-check the running
  process against a stable device identity (UUID/serial) immediately after
  launch. Same shape as the provenance self-check: the cheap confirmation
  belongs at the moment of the claim, not at the moment someone gets
  suspicious.
